<header><p class="st-kicker">other · Iberia</p><h1 class="st-title">Iberian Union</h1><p class="st-meta">1580 CE – 1640 CE · capital Madrid</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The 60 years in which Spain's Habsburg kings also ruled Portugal and its empire, bringing the whole Iberian Peninsula under one crown.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sebastian-of-portugal">King Sebastian of Portugal</a> died without heirs at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-alcacer-quibir">Alcácer Quibir</a> in <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-morocco">Morocco</a> in 1578, and his successor, the elderly <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-king-of-portugal">Cardinal Henry</a>, died in 1580. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-ii-of-spain">Philip II of Spain</a> pressed his claim, won the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-alcantara-1580">Battle of Alcântara</a> and was recognised as Philip I of Portugal by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cortes-of-tomar">Cortes of Tomar</a> in 1581.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>It was a personal union: Portugal kept its own laws, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/corte">Cortes</a>, currency and colonial administration, governed by a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viceroy-of-india">viceroy</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/council-of-portugal">Council of Portugal</a>. But union drew Portugal into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain's</a> wars, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> attacked <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/colonial-brazil">Portuguese Brazil</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaspar-de-guzman-count-duke-of-olivares">Count-Duke of Olivares's</a> plans for tax and military integration caused growing resentment.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>On 1 December 1640 Portuguese nobles rose in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lisbon">Lisbon</a> and proclaimed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-iv-of-portugal">Duke of Braganza</a> as John IV. The Restoration War ended with Spanish recognition of Portuguese independence in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-lisbon-1668">Treaty of Lisbon</a> of 1668.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/iberian-union.jpg" alt="Iberian Union" width="320" height="161"><figcaption>Image: Gabriel Ziegler · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:IberianUnionandCouncils.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1580 CE – 1640 CE</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>other</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Madrid</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Philip II, Philip III, Philip IV</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Castilian, Portuguese</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Spain, Portugal</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Philippine Dynasty, União Ibérica</dd></dl></aside></div>

